<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ludowingians</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">11th century–<a href="/years/1247">1247</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a> · Also known as Ludowingian dynasty</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Ludowingians were a German noble dynasty that ruled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thuringia">Thuringia</a> as landgraves from 1131 and also held <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hesse">Hesse</a>. Founded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-vii-duke-of-bavaria">Louis the Bearded</a> in the 11th century, they built the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wartburg">Wartburg</a> and made their court a centre of courtly poetry. Landgrave Louis IV married <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elizabeth-of-hungary">Saint Elizabeth of Hungary</a>, and the line died out with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-raspe">Henry Raspe</a> in 1247, after which Thuringia passed to the Wettins.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ludowingians.jpg" alt="Ludowingians" width="320" height="349" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:COA_family_de_Landgrafen_von_Hessen.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Louis the Bearded came of a noble family whose descent cannot be traced with certainty; like the related Reginbodo family it was close to the Archbishopric of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mainz">Mainz</a> and held estates on the Middle Main. The story that around 1040 he received a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fief">fief</a> north of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thuringian-forest">Thuringian Forest</a> and held the castle of Schauenburg near Friedrichroda rests only on unverifiable sources from Reinhardsbrunn. Around 1080 his sons Louis the Springer and Beringer of Sangerhausen founded the abbey of Schönrain in Main Franconia, and a deed of 1100 names them counts of Schauenburg.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Louis the Springer gained lands on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unstrut">Unstrut</a> through the will of Adelheid, widow of Count Palatine Frederick III, built the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wartburg">Wartburg</a> above <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eisenach">Eisenach</a> and in 1085 founded Reinhardsbrunn, thereafter the family's house monastery. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/investiture-controversy">Investiture Controversy</a> he was one of the leading opponents of Emperor Henry V. His son Louis married Hedwig of Gudensberg, heiress of the Hessian count Giso IV, and after 1137 the inheritance of the Gisonen in North Hesse passed to the family. In 1131 Emperor Lothair made him landgrave, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thuringia">Thuringia</a>, now held directly from the empire, left the Duchy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxony">Saxony</a>.</p><p>Under Louis II and Louis III the landgraviate grew further, and Hermann I resisted an attempt by Emperor Henry VI to take it back as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fief">fief</a>. Louis IV was promised the March of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meissen">Meissen</a> in 1226 but died before he could acquire it. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hesse">Hesse</a> was largely ruled by younger brothers who bore the title of count of Gudensberg; one of them, Conrad Raspe, entered the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/teutonic-order">Teutonic Order</a> in 1234 and soon became its Hochmeister.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-raspe">Henry Raspe</a>, regent for his nephew Hermann II and landgrave after 1241, was elected German antiking in 1246 and died the next year. His nephew Henry, Margrave of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meissen">Meissen</a>, secured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thuringia">Thuringia</a> by the Treaty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/weissenfels">Weißenfels</a> in 1249. Louis IV's daughter Sophia of Brabant fought for the inheritance until her defeat at Besenstedt in 1263; she gave up Thuringia in 1264 but secured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hesse">Hesse</a> for her son Henry, and the Landgraviate of Hesse became an imperial principality in 1291.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ludovingians” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The <b>Ludowingian</b> family, founded by Louis the Bearded, built up lands in Thuringia in the 11th century, building the Wartburg near Eisenach around 1067.</blockquote><a href="/state/landgraviate-of-thuringia">Landgraviate of Thuringia</a></li></ul></section>
